        [--checkpoint NOTE --inclusion-proof PROOF.json --witness-policy POLICY [--held-size N --held-root ROOT |
         --held-state FILE [--write-held-state]] [--consistency-proof PROOF.json] [--key-transition RECORD.json]]

RECORD.json is a lab record's four-field envelope (signature, key_id, signed_payload,
receipt_hash), as POST /v1/execute answers it under "decision" and GET /v1/sanction-requests/:id
under "statement". LAB-KEYS.json is the lab key file (mix requisition.lab.keygen writes it; GET
/v1/keys serves it). HEX is the act hash the checker expects the record to be bound to.

WHAT A LAB RECORD IS, AND WHY THIS NEVER SAYS "VERIFIED". A lab record is a legacy agent_receipt.v1
receipt signed by a lab key: a DEV key held in the app's environment, or a lab KMS key (below).
The best it can be is DEGRADED (lab): a lab key, legacy
format, not a trust root. So a record that passes every check here exits 5 (on the Ed25519 path a CEASE
record, which authorises nothing, exits 0: see EXIT CODES), and the unchanged verifier's exit 0 is never
quoted without this verdict.

THE TWO PATHS, chosen by the lab key file's entry for the record's key_id (its "algorithm"; an entry
without one, or with any other value, is refused: default deny):

  * ed25519 -- the record goes, as read, to the UNCHANGED stdlib verifier
    bench/holytrinity-v2/verifier/verify_receipt.py (released by tag; this file never edits or
    copies it), run as a stranger runs it: python3 -I verify_receipt.py --receipt --registry. Any
    verdict of its other than exit 0 is passed through UNCHANGED, its exit and its words, so its
    codes stay its own (registry_missing_key for a production record checked against the lab key
    file). On its exit 0 the lab checks below run, and a record that passes them exits 5
    lab_dev_key, or lab_kms_key when a lab KMS key signed it (a CEASE record: exit 0).
  * mldsa87 -- verify_receipt.py is NOT run: it checks Ed25519 only, by its own rule (its
    THE RULE THIS FILE BOUNDS), and requires a 32-byte key. Python's standard library has no
    ML-DSA, so this file checks the record's bytes, binding and labels and the key entry's own
    integrity, never the signature, and exits 5 lab_signature_unchecked_mldsa87, saying so. The
    signature is checked by mix requisition.lab.verify_mldsa87 ENVELOPE --keys LAB-KEYS.json (Erlang
    :crypto over OpenSSL 3.5; not an independent implementation).

A record whose key_id the key file does not name, or names twice, goes to the unchanged verifier
on either path: those are its trust questions, answered in its words.

THE KEY RULE. One rule for when a key may sign
(lib/autonomous_agency/authority/execute_api/lab_key_rule.ex is its text; lab/requisition_key_rule_vectors.v1.json
its vectors, run against this file by test/autonomous_agency_web/v1/lab_verify_key_rule_test.exs): a key is refused
if it is compromised or revoked (key_revoked), if its status is not active or retired (key_status_unusable), if its
window is unreadable, if the record's signed occurred_at is unreadable, or if occurred_at is outside
valid_from <= occurred_at < valid_to (key_not_valid_at_signing); a retired key verifies exactly its own window.
This file applies the rule ITSELF, once (apply_key_rule), on both paths and BEFORE the unedited verify_receipt.py,
which does not know a signer and treats a retired key with no valid_to as open-ended (lab_key_revoked,
lab_key_status_not_active, lab_key_window_unreadable, lab_signed_instant_unreadable, lab_key_not_valid_at_signing,
lab_signer_not_accepted; exit 5, a trust refusal); on the Ed25519 path, what the rule passes verify_receipt.py then
judges in its own words and codes. A record signed by a key whose status is "compromised" is therefore refused
lab_key_revoked (exit 5) here, where verify_receipt.py alone answers exit 6. A signed payload this file does not
read as a JSON object gets no key rule; it never passes (checks 4 and 5), and on the Ed25519 path an
answer of verify_receipt.py other than exit 0 is passed through, exit 6 included.
--accept-signer LABEL (repeatable, never empty: an empty accepted-signers set is a configuration error) is the relying
party's policy: a record whose key file entry's signer is not one of the labels is refused lab_signer_not_accepted.
Without it any signer verifies: --accept-signer kms makes KMS custody bound what verifies (the dev
keys are exportable). The inputs are read STRICTLY: a duplicated member, NaN/Infinity, a number outside the
double range, a byte-order mark, bytes that are not UTF-8, nesting deeper than 64 containers or an integer outside
+-(2^53-1) are refused (exit 2); verify_receipt.py is handed only bytes this file has already read.

CO-SIGNATURES. This file checks the Ed25519 signature only. A record that carries an ML-DSA-87
co-signature (a "cosignature" member beside the envelope) or announces one (signed metadata.cosign.status
"signed") is said so on every verdict that passes on the Ed25519 path: "co-signature present, NOT checked by this
tool (use verify_offline --require-pq)". The exit code is unchanged. A caller that checks the co-signature itself (verify_offline.py)
passes --cosignature-checked-by-caller and the line is not printed: it would sit inside that caller's PASS, which did check it.

THE CHECKS, in order (an accusation needs standing, so it waits until the key file names the key):
  1. the inputs: two readable JSON files, the envelope's four fields strings, an act hash given;
  2. the key file's entry for the key_id, exactly one (else the verifier, above), its algorithm;
  3. receipt_hash is the SHA-256 of signed_payload, compared here before anything else accuses the
     record: the unchanged verifier reads the signed occurred_at first, so a one-byte change that
     breaks the JSON or the instant reaches it as a trust refusal (exit 5), and every one-byte change
     of signed_payload must exit 1;
  4. THE KEY RULE (above), then the path's own check: the Ed25519 key material and the verifier
     (ed25519), or the entry's fingerprint, key length and the signature's form (mldsa87);
  5. the signed payload is a JSON object whose receipt_type is a lab kind (requisition.lab.), or an
     effector allow's (receipt_type the act type, subject_type effector, signed
     metadata.record_kind requisition.lab.decision.v0);
  6. its signed metadata carries the four labels every lab record is signed with (profile lab, signer dev,
     n6_profile not_followed, and hands: one of HANDS_VALUES, one-person (G-301) or
     two-account, two-passkey (G-301); any other is lab_label_missing), or, for a record a lab KMS seat
     signed, the same four with signer kms. The stronger hands value must carry two distinct sanctioners
     and two distinct approval_records, and no record may carry hands_evidence (else lab_hands_unsupported);
     a record that passes with it is said to rest on those two signed members, NOT re-derived here and
     NOT two people; the value is never upgraded;
  7. the signed metadata.key_id equals the envelope's key_id, and it is one of the two lab
     dev key ids (LabSigner's seat table; a pin compares the two); or, for signer kms: the key
     file's entry says signer kms, the key id is KMS_KEY_ID_PREFIX[the entry's algorithm] + a
     resource id (LabKms.key_id_prefixes/0; a pin compares the two), and the signed
     metadata.kms_key_arn is a KMS key ARN ending in key/<that resource id>;
  8. the act hash the record binds equals --act-hash, byte for byte: the signed metadata.act_hash;
     for an effector record without one, the signed payload_hash; for a CEASE (verdict in CEASE_VERDICTS), the
     signed metadata.display_digest. A record that is no CEASE and binds none is refused here
     (lab_act_hash_absent);
  9. the signed metadata.act_hash_alg is present and is one of ACT_HASH_ALGS. A record bound
     under a label this file does not know is refused lab_act_hash_alg_unknown, and a record with NO act_hash_alg member
     is refused lab_act_hash_alg_absent: every producer of a record that binds an act writes the member, so a record
     without it is not a server record and says nothing about how its hash was made. A CEASE carries no such member; it
     returns at check 8 before this check, by name, not by falling through. This file takes --act-hash as given and
     never derives one (verify_offline.py does, under sha384+canonical_payload.v3 only), so a known label is all it
     can check.

A RECORD SIGNED BY A LAB KMS KEY is checked exactly as above on its path. A record that passes
is still DEGRADED (lab) and exits 5: on the Ed25519 path with verdict_code lab_kms_key (the
record's signed signer is kms; lab_dev_key is a signer-dev record's only;
a CEASE exits 0 there, lab_recorded_no_authority, as for a dev key),
on the ML-DSA-87 path with lab_signature_unchecked_mldsa87 as for any record there. The message says
ONLY what this file can establish offline: the record was signed by a lab key the
key file given pins as AWS KMS key <kms_key_arn> (signer kms), its signature checked against that
pinned public key (on the ML-DSA-87 path: by mix requisition.lab.verify_mldsa87, not here); that
the private key is held in AWS KMS and never exported is the key file's statement, not checked.

EXIT CODES: 0 (on the Ed25519 path, a CEASE record that passed: RECORDED (no authority),
lab_recorded_no_authority), 5 (a lab record that passed: lab_dev_key, lab_kms_key or
lab_signature_unchecked_mldsa87; or a trust refusal: the verifier's; on either path lab_key_revoked,
lab_key_status_not_active, lab_key_window_unreadable, lab_signed_instant_unreadable, lab_key_not_valid_at_signing
(THE KEY RULE) or lab_signer_not_accepted (--accept-signer); lab_key_not_prime_order (the Ed25519 key material);
lab_key_fingerprint_mismatch or lab_key_public_key_malformed (the ML-DSA-87 entry); or lab_verifier_unavailable),
1 (a refusal of the record, or of an entry whose algorithm is absent or not known: every other lab_ code), 2 (usage),
and whatever else the unchanged verifier answered, passed through (1, 2, 5 or 6). Every exit-0, exit-1 and exit-5
answer of this file, and every exit-1 or exit-5 verdict of the verifier, ends with one line
"verdict_code: <code>". Codes of this file are its own, each lab_-prefixed,
sharing no spelling with the verifier's, a step-0 code or a DenialTaxonomy code.

LOG CHECKPOINTS (bench/holytrinity-v2/verifier/CHECKPOINTS.md). The checkpoint flags are handed, as given, to
